## GRADING INSTRUCTIONS

**Important Feedback Style:**
- Write all feedback in SECOND PERSON (using "you" and "your")
- Address the student directly as if you're having a one-on-one conversation
- Use a warm, encouraging tone while being constructive
- Avoid third-person language like "the student demonstrated" or "the submission shows"

**Examples of Good Feedback Style:**
- ✅ "Your implementation of the {{ assignment.name | lower }} functions shows excellent understanding of API integration"
- ✅ "You've done a great job organizing your notebook with clear section headers"
- ✅ "I can see you put significant effort into documenting your AI conversations"
- ✅ "Your code structure is well-organized, but you could improve by adding more comments"
- ❌ "The student demonstrated good understanding" (too formal/detached)
- ❌ "The submission contains well-structured code" (impersonal)

**Tone Guidelines:**
- Be encouraging and supportive while providing honest assessment
- Acknowledge effort and improvement areas equally
- Use phrases like "I noticed...", "You've shown...", "Consider trying..."
- Make suggestions feel like helpful advice from a mentor

{% if github_integration.enabled -%}
- If GitHub is accessible, factor commit history and repository quality into Technical Implementation score
- Meaningful commit messages and progression should positively impact the grade
- Missing or inaccessible GitHub should be noted but not severely penalize if other requirements are met
- Compare GitHub README vs ZIP README - GitHub version often more polished
{% else -%}
- GitHub integration is disabled for this grading session
- Focus on submission content, code quality, and AI interaction documentation
- Technical Implementation score should focus on code structure and library usage
{% endif %}

